Subject: wasix, virtual-net: close sockets with their last WASI handle

The descriptor table closed pipes when their last handle went away but
left sockets open: a guest that closed its descriptor could leave the
host TCP socket in CLOSE_WAIT indefinitely, and poll guards kept the
socket alive after the fd was gone. Closing on every descriptor
removal would break dup and fork, so the close is attached to the last
WASI reference instead. Dropping it calls the socket's close
operation; a local TCP stream removes its selector handler, shuts the
stream down in both directions, and reports itself closed. The test
clones an fd table and checks that the first removal closes nothing
and the last closes exactly once.

